  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promote Mifumi Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Domestic Violence in Uganda is extremely high, with an estimated 70% of women suffering some form of violence in their lives. More disturbing still is that most women and men believe that domestic violence is a private issue and often a justified reaction to a woman&apos;s behaviour. Despite the high prevalence, conviction rates for the crime remain appallingly low, at less than 6%. Due to poor support services and weak community sanctions against abuse, women who experience domestic violence (DV) and their children remain vulnerable and underserved. The MIFUMI Project was founded in 1998 with the goal of supporting change in just one village in Tororo District in eastern Uganda. Today, MIFUMI is one of the most well respected and influential NGOs in the District and in the country, with a programme that has affected the lives of many thousands of women who are victims of violence. Its work is unique and innovative, working at every level to support women and change the policy and attitudinal environment around them. As a result of MIFUMI&apos;s work, a bye-law and Ordinance on Bride Price, which MIFUMI had shown was directly linked to violence in marriage, has been passed. MIFUMI has also put forward two Constitutional petitions, one on Bride Price and one on polygamy, both of which are issues their research has shown are linked to high levels of domestic violence. As a result of the last investment grant, MIFUMI has trained 42 counsellors and has 21 community-managed Advice Centres supporting women across Tororo. Through this investment, MIFUMI will support women who have survived violence in their lives. More than 200 trained champions will directly support over 11,000 women to avoid abuse, gain economic empowerment and pursue routes out of violence. Additionally, they will work with over 2,400 men who will be engaged to reach out to their communities to change attitudes and behaviours with respect to violence against women. MIFUMI will also launch the Domestic Violence Watch to monitor domestic violence responses and action in Uganda. Finally, they will step up their national level campaigns to change policy and practice on domestic violence. Nationally and internationally, MIFUMI is championing the reform of bride price and an end to polygamy with the long-term aim being to develop a model that is replicated more widely to eradicate domestic violence.</narrative>
  </description>
